I’ll inspect the measurement metadata and diffraction profile first, then identify candidate phases and carry the best structural model through a GSAS-II refinement. I’ll validate the exported CIFs, phase fractions, and profile against the saved project before reporting uncertainty.

The chemistry strongly suggests calcium tungstate formation, but the low synthesis temperature makes residual precursor or an intermediate plausible. I’m now checking the peak inventory and the installed phase-search/indexing interfaces rather than assuming a single-product model.

Peak positions now give unambiguous signatures of monoclinic WO₃ and scheelite CaWO₄; several additional reflections are consistent with residual calcite. I’m separating genuine calcite peaks from overlaps by comparing full profiles and will retain a third phase only if it materially improves the refinement and stays physically stable.

The three-phase trial is already a strong fit after only cell and common profile refinement (weighted profile R ≈ 11.7%), with provisional fractions near 54% WO₃, 38% calcite, and 8% CaWO₄. The refinement is still converging phase-dependent widths; I’m checking those values for physical stability and comparing against reduced models.

The reduced models are decisively worse (Rwp ≈ 16.8–17.2% versus 11.7% for three phases), so calcite and CaWO₄ are both required in addition to WO₃. I also found and removed an exact scale-factor degeneracy in the initial trial; the next comparison uses a constrained, physically interpretable profile and alternative WO₃ structure settings.

The better monoclinic WO₃ model (same γ-WO₃ phase in a nonstandard cell setting) lowers Rwp to 9.5% with a stable, non-singular width model. Fractions are now insensitive to reasonable profile choices—about 54% WO₃, 38.5% calcite, and 7.8% CaWO₄—so I’m checking the remaining residual for texture or missed phase evidence before freezing the final model.

A narrower fixed instrumental contribution plus phase-specific size/strain removes the major width bias and improves Rwp to 8.4%, but that trial stopped before convergence. Its phase fractions moved to roughly 55% WO₃, 33% calcite, and 12% CaWO₄, so I’m continuing it to stability before deciding whether that extra broadening model is justified.

The three-phase model is now stable: monoclinic γ-WO₃, calcite CaCO₃, and scheelite CaWO₄. A modest calcite (104) preferred orientation is supported by the profile and lowers Rwp to about 8.03%; I’m packaging that converged refinement and checking every exported artifact against the saved project.

The refinement reloads correctly with all three phases. I hit a script-interface mismatch while extracting the limits (the project itself is intact), so I’m reading the project tree directly and will use that same saved-state data for the deliverables.

The final quantitative state is 57.33 wt% γ-WO₃, 29.86 wt% calcite, and 12.81 wt% scheelite CaWO₄, normalized across crystalline phases. Before writing the report, I’m checking the rejected two-phase fits so the phase-identification evidence is reported quantitatively and not overstated.

A control comparison exposed an important issue: a triclinic WO₃ reference fits dramatically better than the monoclinic model (Rwp 1.80% versus 8.03%). I’m reopening that refinement now; unless it is being driven by a singular or nonphysical parameter, it supersedes the provisional monoclinic assignment and fractions.

That apparent breakthrough was an artifact: the triclinic trial had imported only 214 observations (10–12.3°) because its temporary XYE file was caught mid-write, so its low Rwp did not describe the full pattern. I found the truncation in the saved project and am rerunning that candidate sequentially over all 8,284 fitted points before locking the WO₃ polymorph.

The corrected full-range triclinic test is decisively poor (Rwp 42.5%) and numerically unstable, so the γ-WO₃ assignment remains the valid one. I’m doing one final residual audit now—looking for systematic unmodeled peaks or line-shape bias—before freezing the project and exporting it.

The numerical audit is still valid; only the plotting library is absent from the GSAS-II environment. I’ll generate the diagnostic with the analysis environment instead and keep the refinement data unchanged.

The residual audit finds no convincing extra reflections, but the largest errors are intensity mismatches within the WO₃ peak groups rather than missing peaks. That points to the choice of WO₃ reference coordinates/intensity model, so I’m screening the local COD WO₃ structures before accepting an 8% fit.

The local COD screen confirms the chosen γ-WO₃ model is uniquely compatible with the full pattern. Its 1960-era coordinates had zero displacement factors; refining physically constrained common W and O displacement terms improves Rwp to 7.88% and keeps both terms positive. I’m testing whether the low-precision tungsten coordinates themselves account for the remaining intensity bias.

A fully generalized anisotropic-strain model lowers Rwp to 6.96%, confirming direction-dependent WO₃ broadening, but it develops nonphysical negative terms and an SVD failure, so I will not submit it. I’m replacing it with a constrained uniaxial broadening test that adds only one independent width parameter and can remain physically stable.

The six constrained width tests are still running in parallel; they are CPU-bound rather than stalled. I’ll collect them from their project files once the refinements finish, then keep only a converged, positive model.

The constrained WO₃ model is now stable at Rwp 7.47%: positive common displacement factors, a single uniaxial microstrain axis, no singularities, and converged shifts below 0.01σ. An added anisotropic-size term improved Rwp by only 0.07% and was 96% correlated with strain, so I rejected it. I’m checking the symmetry-natural c-axis broadening of the two calcium phases as the last model-selection step.

The calcium-phase anisotropy checks do not justify extra parameters: CaWO₄ worsens the fit, and calcite changes Rwp by only 0.007%. I’m therefore freezing the simpler converged model at Rwp 7.47%, with three phases, calcite (104) texture, and only the supported WO₃ uniaxial strain term.

All requested files have been exported from `final.gpx`. I’m now doing the handoff checks: reopening the final project, independently parsing each CIF, verifying composition/cell agreement, and checking that `fit.csv` reproduces the stored Rwp and that the JSON fractions sum exactly to one.

